---
title: "Ecosystem — FrontierJS docs"
description: "The FrontierJS ecosystem: which packages make up the framework, how they depend on each other, and what runs an app once it ships."
render: static
---
<!--
  Ecosystem: everything around an app. What an app IS is the Concepts page.
  The six rings and their order are fli ws:atlas's (packages/cli/core/repo-rings.js),
  so this page and the atlas tell one story; a package that moves ring there
  moves section here. Each package's state is its own page under /packages/.
-->
<script>
  import DocsShell from '../../components/DocsShell.mesa'

  export let data = null

  const toc = [
    { href: '#rings', title: 'The rings' },
    { href: '#spine', title: 'Spine' },
    { href: '#substrate', title: 'Substrate' },
    { href: '#batteries', title: 'Batteries' },
    { href: '#conduits', title: 'Conduits' },
    { href: '#tooling', title: 'Tooling' },
    { href: '#running', title: 'Running it' },
    { href: '#state', title: 'Where each stands' },
  ]
</script>

<DocsShell nav={data?.nav} current="/docs/ecosystem/" {toc}>
<article id="top" class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs · Ecosystem</p>
  <h1>What sits around an app.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    <a href="/docs/concepts/">Concepts</a> is what an app is. This page is everything
    around it: the packages it is built from, the direction they depend in, and what
    runs it once it ships.
  </p>

  <!-- ─── The rings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="rings">
    <h2>Read it center out</h2>
    <p>
      FrontierJS is one repository of packages, and they fall into six rings. A ring is
      a reading order, not a layer: start at the center, and stop reading once you
      have what your task needs.
    </p>
    <ol class="rings">
      <li><a href="#spine">The spine</a><span>One package per realm. Learn these and you know what every other package plugs into.</span></li>
      <li><a href="#substrate">The substrate</a><span>What the spine stands on. Read one when a spine package reaches for it.</span></li>
      <li><a href="#batteries">The batteries</a><span>Optional pieces an app opts into. Nobody needs all of them on day one.</span></li>
      <li><a href="#conduits">The conduits</a><span>One connector per outside service. Open the one for the vendor you are wiring.</span></li>
      <li><a href="#tooling">The tooling</a><span>How an app is made, checked and shipped. You run these long before you read them.</span></li>
      <li><a href="#running">The frontier</a><span>What is built on all of it, including what runs your app in production.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Spine ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="spine">
    <h2>The spine: one package per realm</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/litestone/">Litestone</a></td><td>Data</td><td>The schema language and the database client: tables, migrations, validation, gates, encryption, the audit trail. SQLite underneath.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/junction/">Junction</a></td><td>API</td><td>Services, the hook pipeline, and HTTP and WebSocket transport over a Litestone database.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/sierra/">Sierra</a></td><td>UI</td><td>The app framework over Mesa: resources, forms, routing, and builds for a web app, a static site, a widget or an extension.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Dependencies point one way, toward the data. Junction imports Litestone; Sierra
      imports both. Nothing imports back up the chain, which is why the data rules hold
      no matter which realm a call starts in.
    </p>
<pre class="figure" aria-label="Dependency direction">Litestone  ←  Junction  ←  Sierra
                              ↓
                            Mesa</pre>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Substrate ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="substrate">
    <h2>The substrate: what the spine stands on</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/mesa/">Mesa</a></td><td>The language every interface is written in: a compiler and a small runtime. It imports nothing from the spine, so it can be used on its own.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/css/">@frontierjs/css</a></td><td>The styling language. You style with a tone, like <code>danger</code>, and a treatment, like <code>outlined</code>, never with a color.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/toolbelt/">Toolbelt</a></td><td>Pure functions with no dependencies and no I/O: dates, money and units, the query directives. Any package may import it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Batteries ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="batteries">
    <h2>The batteries: opt in to what you need</h2>
    <p>Each battery has one job and attaches at one seam.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>What it adds</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/auth/">auth</a></td><td>Sign-in, sessions, password reset, email verification and API keys</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/caravan/">Caravan</a></td><td>Jobs, queues and schedules, stored in SQLite</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/conduit/">Conduit</a></td><td>Third-party integrations in both directions: calls out and webhooks in</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/notifications/">notifications</a></td><td>A notification defined once and delivered over whichever transports the app configures</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/orion/">Orion</a></td><td>Automations: flows of triggers, conditions and actions</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/mcp/">mcp</a></td><td>An MCP server for AI agents, derived from the schema, with the gate as its permissions</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/testing/">testing</a></td><td>One test environment across Data and API</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/ui/">ui</a></td><td>Mesa components: forms, display, layout, overlays, feedback</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/email-kit/">email-kit</a> · <a href="/packages/print-kit/">print-kit</a></td><td>Email built from Mesa components; a document rendered to PDF</td></tr>
          <tr><td>site-kit</td><td>The engine a marketing site runs on, this one included</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/jetty/">Jetty</a></td><td>The browser extension surface</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Two kinds are worth telling apart. A <strong>Kit</strong> is parts one realm
      composes: ui, email-kit, print-kit and site-kit are all UI. A
      <strong>Rig</strong> is installed across realms: auth, notifications and Orion
      each bring a schema fragment, a service and a resource, and an app takes any
      subset of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Conduits ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="conduits">
    <h2>The conduits: one connector per vendor</h2>
    <p>
      Conduit runs both ways: your app calls a vendor out through it, and the vendor's
      webhooks come back in through it, verified before your code sees them. Each
      conduit is a small package over <a href="/packages/conduit/">Conduit</a> that
      knows one outside service from both ends: its sign-in, the requests you send it,
      the webhooks it sends you and their signatures. Open the one you need; none
      depends on another.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Mail</td><td>Gmail, Mailgun, Postmark, Resend, SendGrid</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Messages and push</td><td>Twilio, Telnyx, Quo, Slack, Mattermost, Apple Push</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Payments and shipping</td><td>Stripe, EasyPost</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AI</td><td>Anthropic, OpenAI</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Identity</td><td>OpenID Connect, Microsoft, Google</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Cloudflare, DigitalOcean, Hetzner, S3, Turnstile</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Data and automation</td><td>Airtable, Basecamp, n8n, Zapier, Plausible, DataForSEO</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Most are prototypes, driven against a stand-in for the vendor, and each is held
      out of releases until it is published on its own.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Tooling ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="tooling">
    <h2>The tooling: make, check, ship</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/fli/"><code>fli</code></a></td><td>The command line. It scaffolds an app, runs it in development, checks it, migrates it, and deploys and releases it.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/create-frontier/"><code>npm create frontier</code></a></td><td>Starts a new app. It runs <code>fli new</code>, so there is one definition of what an app looks like.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/config/">config</a></td><td>The TypeScript and Biome settings an app extends in one line.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/packages/vscode/">The editor extension</a></td><td>Language support for <code>.lite</code> and <code>.mesa</code> files. Not yet published.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── Running it ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="running">
    <h2>Running it: Basecamp and Outpost</h2>
    <p>
      Once an app ships, it is a <strong>Release</strong>: one built, unchangeable
      version, deployed as a whole. Two pieces move Releases onto servers and keep them
      running.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/basecamp/">Basecamp</a></strong> is the control plane. It
        provisions servers, ships releases, installs the services your apps depend on,
        and shows what is running where. It is itself a FrontierJS app, the largest one
        built so far.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><a href="/packages/outpost/">Outpost</a></strong> is what runs on each
        server. It answers Basecamp's signed commands (pull, build, deploy, stop, check
        health) and reports back. It is not a FrontierJS app, so a server carries no
        schema or database just to run containers.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="from">See it: <a href="/tour/loop/">The loop</a>, scaffold to deploy as one flow.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── State ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="state">
    <h2>Where each package stands</h2>
    <p>
      FrontierJS is pre-alpha, and the packages are not equally far along. Each
      package states its own standing in one line, shipped, working or prototyped,
      followed by the fact that standing rests on.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a checkout, <code>fli ws:atlas --as=rings</code> draws every package in its
      ring with its realm and state, read from the workspace rather than written down.
    </p>
    <p class="from">Every package: <a href="/packages/">Packages</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <footer class="next">
    <p><strong>Next:</strong> <a href="/docs/concepts/">Concepts</a>, what an app is. <a href="/packages/">Packages</a>, one page per package.</p>
  </footer>
</article>
</DocsShell>

